issue.description:
Refine the App shells preview so every shell uses one standardized shadcn Sidebar pattern, the main/right panel uses one consistent abstract content layout, and all shell previews stack vertically at a restrained width.

Overall direction:

* Standardize all App shell examples around the current Classic workspace sidebar treatment.
* Make fuller use of the accepted shadcn Sidebar capabilities instead of hand-building simplified sidebar chrome.
* Standardize the right/main panel structure using the owner's reference.
* Stack all shell examples vertically.
* Do not place Inset project and Compact utility side-by-side.
* Keep each shell narrower than the full preview width; target a visually balanced width around roughly 70% of the available panel, but treat this as implementation guidance rather than a strict percentage.
* Use a consistent, balanced landscape proportion/height so the previews do not become overly tall.

1. Standardize the sidebar

Use the Classic workspace sidebar as the shared baseline for every shell preview.

Preserve/use the accepted shadcn Sidebar system and its existing components, including where appropriate:

* Sidebar;
* SidebarHeader;
* SidebarContent;
* SidebarGroup;
* SidebarMenu;
* SidebarMenuItem;
* SidebarMenuButton;
* SidebarFooter;
* SidebarInset;
* existing Sidebar provider/layout pieces already accepted in the project.

Do not invent separate custom sidebar architectures for the different shell previews.

Make fuller use of shadcn Sidebar capabilities:

* Use a workspace/logo/account switcher style Select/Dropdown in the sidebar header rather than a static logo/name treatment, similar to common shadcn sidebar patterns.
* Use grouped navigation sections.
* Use active navigation state.
* Use icons consistently.
* Use a footer/profile area where it improves completeness.
* Use existing Sidebar variants/states already supported by the project where they work inside contained previews.

Important boundary:

* Do not change the public Sidebar API solely to support these previews.
* Reuse existing accepted Sidebar capabilities and non-collapsible/contained behavior where necessary.
* True desktop collapse remains optional unless already cleanly supported in the preview context.

2. Standardize the right/main panel

Use one consistent abstract main-panel layout inspired by the owner's reference.

Target structure:

* simple top header/breadcrumb row;
* a row of a few muted rectangular placeholder cards near the top;
* one larger muted content block below;
* neutral silhouette/skeleton treatment only.

The right panel should stay intentionally abstract.

Do not build:

* full dashboards;
* forms;
* real tables;
* charts;
* workflow-heavy content.

Use semantic tokens such as muted/card/border/background so the placeholders still respond to theme changes.

3. Keep shell examples distinct without changing sidebar architecture

The examples may still differ through restrained shell/layout treatment such as:

* spacing density;
* active nav selection;
* content inset/frame treatment;
* header/breadcrumb treatment;
* subtle border/surface differences;
* content proportions;
* compact vs roomy spacing.

But the underlying sidebar structure/pattern should remain the standardized Classic workspace implementation.

4. Vertical page layout

Stack shell previews in a single vertical sequence.

Example order can remain:

* Classic workspace;
* Inset project;
* Compact utility.

Do not render two shell examples side-by-side.

For each shell:

* keep a consistent preview width and landscape proportion where practical;
* do not stretch to the full available preview width;
* center or align the previews cleanly within the App shells panel;
* allow the worker to tune the exact width from browser evidence, with \~70% used only as a visual target.

At narrower widths:

* shell previews may expand closer to full width to avoid awkward cramped layouts;
* preserve responsive containment and avoid page-level horizontal overflow.

5. Live theme/sidebar linkage

All shell previews must continue to reflect live Personal UI theme state:

* semantic colors;
* typography;
* spacing/radius;
* border/shadow where relevant;
* light/dark mode;
* sidebar token OFF/ON;
* dedicated sidebar token values.

When dedicated sidebar tokens are OFF:

* use the accepted base alias behavior.

When dedicated sidebar tokens are ON:

* use the dedicated sidebar token set.

6. Interaction

Keep lightweight shell interaction:

* workspace/logo switcher Select/Dropdown may be interactive;
* active navigation may be selectable;
* hover/focus states should work;
* no full app workflows are needed.

Interaction must not materially destabilize the shell preview geometry.

7. Visual quality

Aim for:

* clean, polished, believable app chrome;
* strong consistency across the three shells;
* fuller use of shadcn Sidebar patterns;
* restrained abstract content;
* portfolio-quality presentation;
* clear hierarchy without visual noise.

Do not:

* add duplicate theme controls;
* create a second theme state;
* hard-code raw colors where semantic tokens exist;
* add unrelated public resources;
* change Theme v3, persistence, history, Design Command, or mutation semantics;
* create a new sidebar framework.

Accessibility / responsiveness:

* preserve top-level App shells tab semantics;
* preserve keyboard/focus behavior for Select/Dropdown, nav items, and sidebar controls;
* keep shell previews readable at narrow widths;
* preserve logical reading order;
* avoid horizontal overflow.

Acceptance:

* All App shell examples use the standardized Classic workspace shadcn Sidebar pattern.
* Sidebar header makes fuller use of shadcn capabilities, including a workspace/logo/account switcher style Select/Dropdown rather than a static logo-only treatment.
* Shells use grouped navigation, active states, icons, and footer/profile treatment where appropriate.
* Main/right panel structure is standardized to header/breadcrumb + small muted blocks + one larger muted content block.
* Main-panel content remains abstract/skeleton-like.
* Classic workspace, Inset project, and Compact utility are stacked vertically.
* No shell pair is laid out side-by-side.
* Shell previews are narrower than full width at normal desktop sizes, with exact width tuned from browser evidence rather than a hard-coded percentage requirement.
* Shell previews use a balanced landscape proportion and avoid excessive height.
* Live theme/sidebar token changes continue to update every shell.
* No public Sidebar API change or parallel sidebar framework is introduced.
* No responsive, accessibility, persistence, history, Design Command, or theme regression.
